<header><p class="enc-kicker">Institution</p><h1 class="enc-title">Serbian Patriarchate of Peć</h1><p class="enc-meta"><span class="dates"><a href="/years/1346">1346</a>–<a href="/years/1766">1766</a></span> · <a href="/centuries/14th-century">14th century</a></p></header>
<div class="enc-body"><p class="enc-desc">The Serbian Patriarchate of Peć was the patriarchate of the Serbian <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a>, seated at the Patriarchal Monastery of Peć in present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/kosovo">Kosovo</a>. The Serbian archbishopric was raised to a patriarchate in 1346, when <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> was crowned emperor; after lapsing under Ottoman rule it was restored in 1557 with the support of the grand <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/vizier">vizier</a> Mehmed Sokolović, and abolished again in 1766. It sustained Serbian church life under the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a>.</p><figure class="enc-fig"><img src="/images/encyclopedia/serbian-patriarchate-of-pec.jpg" alt="Serbian Patriarchate of Peć" width="320" height="208" fetchpriority="high"><figcaption>Image: Vladimir Vukotic · CC BY-SA 3.0 · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Manastir_Pecka_Patrijarsija_2.jpg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Wikimedia Commons</a></figcaption></figure></div>
<section class="enc-more"><h2 class="enc-more-h">Origins</h2><p>From 1219 the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/eastern-orthodox-church">Orthodox Church</a> of the Kingdom of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbia">Serbia</a> was an autocephalous archbishopric, seated first at the Žiča Monastery and from the middle of the 13th century at Peć. After King <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/stefan-dusan">Stefan Dušan</a> conquered northern <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greece">Greece</a>, including <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serres">Serres</a>, he was proclaimed emperor there on 25 December 1345. Because an emperor was customarily crowned by a patriarch, a state and church assembly met at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> on 16 April 1346, raised the archbishopric to a patriarchate, and the new Patriarch Joanikije II crowned Dušan Emperor of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/greeks">Greeks</a>. The patriarchate took over <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/mount-athos">Mount Athos</a> and many Greek eparchies in Aegean Macedonia.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">History</h2><p>Because the change was made without the consent of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>, Patriarch Callistus I excommunicated Dušan and Joanikije II in 1350. Facing the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> after the Battle of Maritsa, the two churches agreed in 1375 to revoke the act and recognise the patriarchate, on condition that the contested southern eparchies returned to Constantinople. The Ottomans took the monastery of Peć in 1455, and after Patriarch Arsenije II died in 1463 the office fell vacant, while the Archbishopric of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ohrid">Ohrid</a> took over its eparchies. Metropolitan Pavle of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/smederevo">Smederevo</a> briefly seized the throne of Peć, but his movement was crushed and he went into exile after 1541.</p><p>The patriarchate was restored in 1557, when Makarije, a cousin of the Ottoman statesman Mehmed Sokolović, was elected patriarch. It grew to more than 40 eparchies, from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/skopje">Skopje</a> to <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/buda">Buda</a>. Patriarch Arsenije III sided with the Austrians in 1689 during the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-turkish-war">Great Turkish War</a>, and Ottoman reprisals led to the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/great-migration-african-american">Great Migration</a> of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a>; Arsenije IV likewise backed the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/habsburg-dynasty">Habsburgs</a> in the war of 1737–1739. The <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/ottoman-empire">Ottomans</a> then favoured Greek bishops, beginning with Joannicius III in 1739, and after 1752 Serbian and Greek claimants fought over the throne until Sultan Mustafa III abolished it and placed its eleven remaining eparchies under <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/constantinople">Constantinople</a>.</p><h2 class="enc-more-h">Legacy</h2><p>For <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/serbs">Serbs</a> under Ottoman rule the restored patriarchate was a religious and ethnic symbol that stood in for their lost state. The historian Frederick Anscombe has argued that its offices had no ethnic monopoly in the Ottoman era, yet most of the patriarchs were Serbs who kept the title of successors of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/saint-sava">Saint Sava</a>.</p><p class="enc-more-src">This account follows the Wikipedia article “Serbian Patriarchate of Peć” (CC BY-SA 4.0), linked under Read further below.</p></section>
<section class="enc-quotes"><h2 class="enc-section-title">Across the Institute</h2><ul><li><blockquote>…could turn a Christian village boy into a vizier: Sokollu Mehmed Pasha, born to a Serbian Orthodox family in Herzegovina, governed the empire as grand vizier for fourteen years, persuaded the sultan to restore the <b>Serbian Patriarchate of Peć</b> and placed relatives, Muslim and Christian, in high posts.</blockquote><a href="/societies/ottoman-empire">Social Hierarchy of the Ottoman Empire</a></li><li><blockquote>Vuk ruled from Priština and Vučitrn, drawing income from the silver mines of Trepča and Novo Brdo and protecting the <b>Patriarchate of Peć</b>.</blockquote><a href="/state/district-of-brankovic">District of Branković</a></li></ul></section>
